2382:## D174 · 2026-10-25 · Three-document end-to-end concurrency diagnostic
2383-
2384-- Alex asked to keep going. The [registered comparison](research/2026-10-25-ingestion-smoke/plan.md) uses the whole SK, NAVER and follow-up documents from July 24 against the ordered pre-July-24 baseline. It runs through the product's replay path on separate scratch ledgers, with fresh archives, three serial controls and three concurrent trials; the first control is read before the next trial. This is diagnostic, not production acceptance.
2385-- The study shares at most two provider requests across all stages. It keeps the $1.50 total conservative ceiling and a 144-call ceiling, stops on failure, and does not alter production prompts, grouping rules, default transports or source/gold databases. Reservation now uses the existing request's actual output cap while retaining the full model input bound and conservative published rates.
2386-- Before paid calls, the snapshot was corrected for this study to explicitly omit unordered sequence-zero audit rows; the source records are retained untouched. The audit rebuilds each accepted request from its committed prefix and rechecks that exact archived answer into the saved handover. Worker-side schema checks close admission before releasing a slot, including during daily judging. Free full-path controls and a substituted-handover mutant pass/fail as intended; 156 tests passed. Read-only design and code follow-up reviews found no serious issue.
2387-- The first live serial control completed structurally but failed the NAVER-project check. The model expressed the 2028 expansion as capacity, which the numeric horizon refused; the older library expressed it as an event and accepted it. Evidence and its group disappeared with the candidate. The [result and census](../poc/reports/2026-10-25-ingestion-smoke/results.md) record the trace. No concurrent live trial followed; the journal is closed. Eligibility and the gold remain unchanged pending a product decision. This is not a concurrency acceptance failure or a measured speed regression.
